# 13. INCLUSIVE DESIGN AND ACCESSIBILITY

Inclusivity is a core design requirement, not an optional feature.

Design the game for children with different communication, sensory, learning, and motor needs.

Include:

- Simple and short instructions.
- Clear language.
- Large buttons.
- Large and recognizable pictograms.
- High-contrast text and controls.
- Consistent visual placement of important controls.
- Clear labels for every icon.
- Visual feedback for actions.
- Optional narration or text-to-speech where browser support allows.
- The ability to repeat instructions.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Visible keyboard focus.
- Subtitles for spoken information.
- Adjustable text size.
- Reduced-motion mode.
- Independent sound and music controls.
- No flashing effects.
- No mandatory time pressure.
- No information communicated through color alone.

Use pictograms alongside text for important decisions.

Each pictogram must represent one clear concept and be easy to understand.

Do not overcrowd the screen.

The player must be able to understand the objective and available choices without needing to read long paragraphs.

Use accessible HTML semantics and appropriate labels.

Do not make sound, color perception, fast reactions, or complex reading a requirement for completing a challenge.

